		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Réponse à&#160;:  ecpg : indicateur (variable indicatrice) et type varchar]]></title>
		<link rel="alternate" href="http://forums.postgresql.fr/viewtopic.php?pid=22504#p22504"/>
		<content type="html"><![CDATA[<p>Bonjour,</p><p>La réponse de la part de Michael Meskes :<br />&quot;<br />&gt; Why the indicator equals 0 (is not strictly positive) and the value is<br />&gt; truncated (modified) ?</p><p>Because according to standard C types it is not truncated. The string does<br />have more digits tha a flot can handle, but they are all valid, i.e. a<br />convesion does correctly eat them all but the internal representation is not<br />able to store them all.</p><p>You bring up an interesting point here, the data is not exactly truncated, it<br />is just rounded. Not sure what (if anything) the standard says.</p><p>&gt; Why this error message whereas an example is given in the PostgreSQL 9.4<br />&gt; documentation (see above) ?</p><p>I guess the documentation is wrong, but I have to look into it. Normally the<br />varchar type needs a length (as an array of char) or it will become a pointer<br />to char.<br />&quot;</p>]]></content>
